# example_data/

This directory is where the notebook reads data from. On first run, if no
real detection data is present, `load_data("example_data/")` generates a
synthetic detection dataset (random colored rectangles on noise backgrounds)
and caches it under `example_data/_cache/`. Subsequent runs reload from the
cache.

The synthetic data is enough to verify the pipeline executes end-to-end; it
is NOT a meaningful detection benchmark. To run on real detection data, drop
one of the formats below into this directory.

## Use your own data

`load_data` checks formats in this priority order:
`.pt` → COCO-format directory → synthetic fallback.

### Option 1 — `.pt` (fastest)

A single `.pt` file (any name) containing a dict:

```python
torch.save({
    "images_train":  [PIL_image, PIL_image, ...],   # list of PIL.Image.Image
    "targets_train": [target_dict, target_dict, ...],
    "images_test":   [PIL_image, ...],
    "targets_test":  [target_dict, ...],
}, "example_data/my_dataset.pt")
```

Each `target_dict` has the format:

```python
{
    "boxes":  torch.tensor([[x1, y1, x2, y2], ...], dtype=torch.float),  # xyxy
    "labels": torch.tensor([class_idx, ...], dtype=torch.long),
}
```

If a target dict has no boxes (image with no objects), pass empty tensors:
`boxes=torch.zeros((0, 4), dtype=torch.float)` and
`labels=torch.zeros((0,), dtype=torch.long)`.

If you want to override the bundled synthetic fallback, name your file so it
sorts first alphabetically, or delete `_cache/synthetic_detection.pt`.

### Option 2 — COCO-format directory

A directory (any name) containing:

```
my_coco_subset/
├── images/                    # all image files (.jpg, .png, etc.)
└── annotations.json           # COCO-format JSON
```

The `annotations.json` follows the standard COCO format with top-level keys
`images`, `annotations`, and `categories`. The loader:

- maps each annotation to its image via `image_id`
- converts COCO `[x, y, w, h]` boxes to `[x1, y1, x2, y2]`
- samples `train_size + n_test` images deterministically (seeded shuffle)
  and splits the result

Place the directory directly inside `example_data/`. The loader scans for
this layout at the top level and one level deep.

### Option 3 — Synthetic fallback (no setup)

If neither a `.pt` file nor a COCO directory is present, the loader generates
a synthetic detection dataset on first run and caches it under
`_cache/synthetic_detection.pt`. The cache embeds the generation parameters
(`train_size`, `n_test`, `seed`); changing any of those triggers
regeneration.

The synthetic generator produces 64×64 RGB images with 1-3 colored rectangles
each, labeled with random class indices in `[0, 5)`. Sufficient for verifying
the pipeline executes; not a real evaluation dataset.

## Note on smoke vs. paper-scale

Detection KD papers typically train on COCO (118K train images, 5K val).
Demo-scale here is 200/50 by default — fast enough to run on a laptop CPU
in minutes, large enough to verify the distillation loop converges directionally.
For paper-faithful results, the researcher must download full COCO and
adjust `train_size` and `n_test` upward in the notebook's `params` block.
